  "reachability": "SQLite stores NaN as NULL and the column is NOT NULL with CHECK(active_seconds >= 0), so the real ledger cannot hold a NaN reservation; the defect is in the code's own refusal claim, not in the live data path",
  "report_claim": "the r7 report lists 'non_finite' and 'overflow_timestamp' among the refused states; no test in tests/v9/experiments covers either and a NaN reservation or a finite far-future timestamp is accepted, not refused",
  "why": "run_conservative_wall_seconds normalises the reservation with max(0.0, float(value)), and Python's max(0.0, nan) is 0.0, so a NaN (or -inf) reservation silently becomes 0 and the following isfinite() guard can never fire for it; the row is then charged only its terminal interval"
